# credgate v1 -- the matched-reward control was masked to the WRONG HALF.
# Every AB-minus-keep contrast in this run is invalid. 2026-09-18, verify PASS.
#
# THE BUG: the half was keyed off the PHASE (upper = !relearning) rather than the
# TARGET. silences-the-wrong-side measured mirror-image profiles -- a LOW target
# works by suppressing the UPPER half, a HIGH one the LOWER. The keep arm's second
# lesson targets A's own f1 320, a LOW target wanting the UPPER half, but
# relearning was true so it was masked to the LOWER. The control was confined to
# the half its own target does not use, in every masked arm.
#
# The data shows it plainly: keep degrades 0.8685 -> 0.9966 (oracle) / 0.9448
# (derived) / 1.0531 (shuffled). So every gap closure here is the control being
# crushed, including the oracle's +5.4 SE and the shuffled arm's +5.3 SE.
# THE 'A COIN FLIP WORKS AS WELL AS THE ORACLE' READING IS RETRACTED -- it is an
# artefact of a broken control, not a finding about masking.
#
# WHAT SURVIVES, and it is the more interesting half: THE AGREEMENT ROW.
#   oracle   1.000  (by construction)
#   derived  0.720  <- the creature's own ear-EMA index
#   shuffled 0.477  (chance)
# The derived index's binarised context agrees with the live phase 72% of the
# time against a 47.7% coin flip. That is real information about which lesson is
# live, and it is measured independently of the retention contrast. It does not
# contradict ctxretain's 0.23/0.23 -- different statistic, slot occupancy vs
# argmax agreement -- but it does say the index is not empty.
#
# Also worth keeping: the oracle's gap is +0.0138 +/- 0.0147 (+0.9 SE), i.e. the
# wipe is GONE under a perfect mask. That direction is right even though the
# magnitude is contaminated by the broken control.

